I had this exact same problem, the problem may be one of the two.

 1) the default file you created is not in the correct format or you have a
    incorrect value in for one of the fields.

 2) the daemon must be started after you have a correct/acceptable default
    player file.

I struggled w/ this for a 2 days, I finally got someone elses default player
file off of the net, and it worked. Thank You grady@fx.com (Steven Grady) & 
doug@letni.lonestar.org (Doug Davis)

Once I used these guys default file it worked, so from my experience it does 
not only take a default player file but a default player file w/ CORRECT 
values to get the daemon to respond. (Note: and it just isn't relying on hit
points alone because mine was jacked up to 50:50 and still did not work)


